{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-72782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-72782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-72782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5237b8dd6da305feb1e85f18f32440b1b7078552e4830a15f8542a76deafe7d1"} {"question" "q-72782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-72782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{90}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={120}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={45}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-72782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={90}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})=41.810{…}^{∘}≈{41.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${41.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/bc0c031fe6ccbb40dc77474582b301456f537ee2a649ba2d5b3bca9b1240618f.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{41.81}^{∘} \\\\  & ={138.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5237b8dd6da305feb1e85f18f32440b1b7078552e4830a15f8542a76deafe7d1"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x={28}^{∘},{132}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={32}^{∘},{128}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={42}^{∘},{158}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={52}^{∘},{138}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5237b8dd6da305feb1e85f18f32440b1b7078552e4830a15f8542a76deafe7d1"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x={180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={270}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={45}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-120866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={90}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.7,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.7)=-44.427\\,004{…}^{∘}≈-{44.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{44.4270}^{∘}$ is not in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-{44.4270}^{∘}.$ To do this, we simply add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{44.4270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={315.5730}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/f3d2d41e1a58dd562ed47ae7e5fcdc79382c426700f520a1153111bfcf2d734b.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{44.4270}^{∘} \\\\  & ={224.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{224.4}^{∘},\\,x≈{315.6}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5237b8dd6da305feb1e85f18f32440b1b7078552e4830a15f8542a76deafe7d1"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "224.4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-332216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "315.6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.6,$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.6)=-36.869\\,897…≈-36.8699\n$$\n\nSince $-{36.8699}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{36.8699}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={36.8699}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={36.8699}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/8d88b4ccfe08c26c9d56effaf651e04703995ffaec4b7ab08187f62ddd1897de.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{36.8699}^{∘} \\\\  & ={216.8699}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘} & ={216.8699}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{143.1301}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-{143.1}^{∘},\\,x≈-{36.9}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5237b8dd6da305feb1e85f18f32440b1b7078552e4830a15f8542a76deafe7d1"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-143.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"} {"question" "q-336283" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-36.9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/state.json" "file_sha256" "e63dc9c75f4d8c8b128bdd6e1f223c7f8571220f94ed04177d5d7564415dbbd7"}] "usage" {} "basis" 813 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
